    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\n`gh auth status` reports SSH as the configured protocol for git operations, and `hosts.yml` contains no token. This creates a false impression that removing SSH keys and revoking PATs is sufficient to de-authorize a machine. In reality, `gh` stores its OAuth token (`gho_*`) silently in the system Keychain under `gh:github.com`, which survives all of those steps.\n\nA user who wants to fully revoke local access has no visible indicator that the Keychain entry exists or that `gh auth logout` is the only reliable way to remove it.\n\n### Affected version\n\nIt's designed this way since 2023 (v2.32.1 specifically).\n\n### Steps to reproduce the behavior\n\n1. Run `gh auth login` with SSH as the git protocol\n2. Confirm `hosts.yml` has no `oauth_token` field - only user/protocol metadata\n3. Revoke all PATs at github.com/settings/tokens\n4. Remove SSH keys from `~/.ssh/`\n5. Run `gh gist list` or any API-backed command\n6. Observe that it still authenticates successfully\n\n### Expected vs actual behavior\n\n**Expected:** Either `gh auth status` surfaces the Keychain-stored token clearly (e.g. `✓ Token stored in: system keychain (gh:github.com)`), or the security log / PAT revocation flow warns that a separate CLI OAuth token may still be active on the machine.\n\n**Actual:** The token is invisible to the user. `gh auth status` only shows `Token: gho_****` without indicating where it comes from. Nothing in GitHub's security UX (PAT revocation, SSH key removal, `hosts.yml` inspection) reveals the token or prompts the user to revoke it via `gh auth logout`.\n\n### Logs\n\n```shell\n❯ cat ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml\ngithub.com:\n    git_protocol: ssh\n    users:\n        ruyrocha:\n    user: ruyrocha\n\n❯ security find-generic-password -s \"gh:github.com\" -w\ngo-keyring-base64:[TOKEN]\n\n❯ gh gist list | head -1\n# still works after removing SSH keys and revoking all PATs\n```\n\n### Related issues\n\n- #13111 - `gh auth logout` does not erase the OAuth token from macOS Keychain. Related but distinct: assumes the user already knows to run `gh auth logout`. This issue is about the prior step — nothing surfaces that the Keychain token exists in the first place.\n- #13317 - `gh api` silently sends unauthenticated requests when Keychain lookup fails. Related but distinct: concerns a broken auth path; this issue concerns a working but invisible auth path that survives all conventional de-authorization steps.\n- #10108 - silent fallback to insecure storage with no warning to the user. Same area of auth transparency.\n- #9233 - old OAuth tokens not revoked on re-auth, leaving stale credentials in the user's account.",
